WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:03.315
Electricity grids run at a nominal frequency:

2
00:00:03.345 --> 00:00:04.745
fifty hertz in Europe.

3
00:00:04.775 --> 00:00:08.905
When generation falls short of consumption, the frequency drops.

4
00:00:08.935 --> 00:00:11.896
When generation exceeds consumption, it rises.

5
00:00:11.926 --> 00:00:15.211
Keeping it steady is the job of ancillary services.

6
00:00:15.687 --> 00:00:20.552
In this episode, we meet three reserves that restore the balance:

7
00:00:20.582 --> 00:00:21.983
FCR, aFRR and mFRR.

8
00:00:23.917 --> 00:00:27.047
Imagine a large power plant suddenly trips offline.

9
00:00:27.077 --> 00:00:32.253
Generation falls below consumption, and within seconds the frequency starts to drop.

10
00:00:32.283 --> 00:00:34.979
If nothing reacted, the fall would continue,

11
00:00:35.009 --> 00:00:38.636
and protection systems could start disconnecting equipment.

12
00:00:39.517 --> 00:00:42.203
To stop the fall and then restore the frequency,

13
00:00:42.233 --> 00:00:45.316
grid operators use reserves that act one after another,

14
00:00:45.346 --> 00:00:49.277
each one slower than the one before, and each one taking over from it.

15
00:00:51.617 --> 00:00:54.778
First comes FCR, the frequency containment reserve.

16
00:00:54.808 --> 00:00:58.909
It reacts automatically, in proportion to the frequency deviation,

17
00:00:58.939 --> 00:01:02.038
and must be fully activated within thirty seconds.

18
00:01:02.068 --> 00:01:06.168
It stops the fall, but does not bring the frequency back to fifty.

19
00:01:06.650 --> 00:01:10.202
Next, aFRR, the automatic frequency restoration reserve.

20
00:01:10.232 --> 00:01:15.512
The operator's controller sends a signal, and the reserve follows it automatically,

21
00:01:15.542 --> 00:01:18.774
reaching full activation within about five minutes.

22
00:01:18.804 --> 00:01:21.205
It brings the frequency back to fifty.

23
00:01:21.683 --> 00:01:25.316
Finally, mFRR, the manual frequency restoration reserve.

24
00:01:25.346 --> 00:01:27.932
Here the operator orders the activation,

25
00:01:27.962 --> 00:01:32.052
with a full activation time of about twelve and a half minutes,

26
00:01:32.082 --> 00:01:35.584
taking over from aFRR when the imbalance lasts longer.

27
00:01:37.280 --> 00:01:41.603
In Europe, these reserves are increasingly shared across borders.

28
00:01:41.633 --> 00:01:46.760
PICASSO is the European platform for aFRR, and MARI is the platform for mFRR,

29
00:01:46.790 --> 00:01:52.251
so operators can use the cheapest available activation offers from many countries.

30
00:01:53.950 --> 00:01:55.866
Providers earn from two things.

31
00:01:55.896 --> 00:01:58.752
First, a capacity payment for being available.

32
00:01:58.782 --> 00:02:02.455
Second, an energy payment when they are actually activated.

33
00:02:02.485 --> 00:02:06.346
Suppose a battery offers ten megawatts of aFRR for four hours,

34
00:02:06.376 --> 00:02:08.668
at eight euros per megawatt per hour.

35
00:02:08.698 --> 00:02:11.932
That is three hundred twenty euros for availability.

36
00:02:12.417 --> 00:02:17.512
If the operator then activates two megawatt-hours at ninety euros per megawatt-hour,

37
00:02:17.542 --> 00:02:20.623
the battery earns another one hundred eighty euros.

38
00:02:20.653 --> 00:02:22.392
In total, five hundred euros.

39
00:02:22.422 --> 00:02:24.284
These numbers are illustrative.

40
00:02:25.980 --> 00:02:29.634
In Southeast Europe, reserve markets are still developing,

41
00:02:29.664 --> 00:02:33.382
and operators are expanding their cross-border cooperation.

42
00:02:33.412 --> 00:02:38.654
Serbia is part of the Continental European synchronous area, and its operator, EMS,

43
00:02:38.684 --> 00:02:43.291
procures reserves, so check the latest EMS publications for requirements,

44
00:02:43.321 --> 00:02:44.498
volumes and prices.

45
00:02:46.180 --> 00:02:47.235
Three takeaways.

46
00:02:47.265 --> 00:02:47.507
One:

47
00:02:47.537 --> 00:02:52.661
FCR reacts automatically within thirty seconds and stops the frequency fall.

48
00:02:52.691 --> 00:02:52.933
Two:

49
00:02:52.963 --> 00:02:57.138
aFRR and mFRR restore the balance, automatically and manually.

50
00:02:57.168 --> 00:02:57.545
Three:

51
00:02:57.575 --> 00:03:01.208
providers earn a capacity payment for being available,

52
00:03:01.238 --> 00:03:03.785
plus an energy payment when activated.

53
00:03:04.280 --> 00:03:07.366
Next, we turn to power futures, forwards and hedging.

54
00:03:07.396 --> 00:03:09.366
Thanks for watching Power Academy.
